NListview.mcc/NListview.mcc

    NAME
        NListview.mcc -- Add a scrollbar container to an NList object.

    SUPERCLASS
        Group.mui

    DESCRIPTION
        This source defines version 19.93. NListview contains an NList
        object (or subclass, including NFloattext), an inner vertical group,
        and two scrollbar objects. Use NList directly when you need
        independent scrollbar management. A standard List object does not
        provide the NList protocol required by this container.

        On AROS, mode resolution forces both scrollbar modes to Always. This
        overrides preferences and explicit None, Auto, FullAuto and Left
        choices. It does not guarantee that both scrollbar objects are
        already attached: layout changes are queued after successful setup,
        and setup itself resolves modes while queuing is disabled. Read the
        dimension attributes after layout to inspect attached scrollbar
        geometry.

        The container is not a general proxy for its child. Retrieve NList
        and address it directly for its complete API. Only the methods and
        notification triggers listed below are forwarded. Other calls go to
        Group; in particular, most NList attribute gets and sets are not
        forwarded.

        The container refuses drag queries. Construction consumes Draggable,
        Dropable and CycleChain tags; Dropable is passed to the child and
        CycleChain is applied to the outer group. A ControlChar supplied at
        construction changes the active entry: Shift moves up, otherwise
        down. The AROS event handler returns zero even after this action.
        Setting ControlChar later does not update the locally stored
        character in this implementation.

        GoActive and GoInactive notify the child through the corresponding
        NList methods, then call the superclass. HandleInput forwards non-
        NONE MUI keys to the child. The legacy MUI 3.8 HandleEvent
        forwarding branch is excluded on AROS.

    METHODS
        Forwarded List methods (MUIM_List_ prefix): Sort, Insert,
        InsertSingle, GetEntry, Clear, Jump, Select, TestPos, Redraw,
        Exchange, Move, NextSelected, Remove, CreateImage, DeleteImage.

        Forwarded NList methods (MUIM_NList_ prefix): Sort, Sort2, Insert,
        InsertSingle, GetEntry, Clear, Jump, Select, TestPos, Redraw,
        RedrawEntry, Exchange, Move, NextSelected, Remove, CreateImage,
        DeleteImage, CopyToClip, UseImage, ReplaceSingle, InsertWrap,
        InsertSingleWrap, GetEntryInfo, GetSelectInfo, CopyTo, DropType,
        DropDraw, DoMethod, ColWidth, ColToColumn, ColumnToCol.

        Forwarded methods retain the child's arguments, return values and
        ownership rules. QueryBeginning is handled locally and returns zero.
        SetScrollers is private; do not call it from application code.

    NOTIFICATIONS
        Notify, KillNotify and KillNotifyObj are forwarded for these
        triggers: List_Prop_Entries, List_Prop_Visible, List_Prop_First,
        List_Active, Listview_SelectChange, Listview_DoubleClick;
        NList_Horiz_Entries, NList_Horiz_Visible, NList_Horiz_First,
        NList_HorizDeltaFactor, NList_Prop_Entries, NList_Prop_Visible,
        NList_Prop_First, NList_VertDeltaFactor, NList_SelectChange,
        NList_MultiClick, NList_DoubleClick, NList_EntryClick, NList_Active,
        NList_First, NList_Entries, NList_TitleClick. Each name has the
        MUIA_ prefix.

        Other notification triggers are handled by the superclass. Scrollbar
        mode setters consume their tags before superclass dispatch, so
        ordinary superclass notifications are not generated for those tags
        by this path.

    NOTES
        Construction modifies the supplied tag list. Child, inner-group and
        scrollbar allocation results are not individually checked; the
        handler does not establish complete recovery from each allocation
        failure. Setup registers its event handler before superclass setup
        and has no local rollback if that setup fails. These limitations are
        not success guarantees.

    SEE ALSO
        MUIA_NListview_NList, MUIA_NListview_Vert_ScrollBar,
        MUIA_NListview_Horiz_ScrollBar

NListview.mcc/MUIA_NListview_Horiz_ScrollBar

    NAME
        MUIA_NListview_Horiz_ScrollBar -- Request the horizontal scrollbar
        mode.

    INPUTS
        Initialize/set/get; LONG. The initial request is
        MUIV_NListview_HSB_Default (5).

    VALUES
        MUIV_NListview_HSB_Always (1): request a persistent scrollbar. Auto
        (2): allow it to appear when needed and remain attached. FullAuto
        (3): allow appearance and removal as needed. None (4): request no
        scrollbar. Default (5): read MUICFG_NListview_HSB, falling back to
        Auto. The abbreviated names use the MUIV_NListview_HSB_ prefix.

        On AROS, every nonzero low-nibble mode is resolved to Always,
        including None. The other choices describe the declared modes, not
        working alternatives on this AROS path.

    FUNCTION
        Set stores the nonzero low nibble as the requested mode and resolves
        the effective mode. Get returns the effective mode, without
        visibility bits; before the first resolution it returns the
        constructor value. A zero low nibble leaves the existing mode
        unchanged.

        MUIV_NListview_HSB_On (0x0300) and HSB_Off (0x0100) are visibility
        request fields used by NList. They may accompany a mode. This
        handler also accepts the vertical visibility field carried by the
        child on this attribute. Off requests are ignored for the effective
        Always mode.

        After successful setup, accepted attachment changes are queued to
        the application and are not synchronous with set. Requests before
        setup are not queued. Setup resolves modes but does not replay an
        attachment request afterward. Do not use this attribute as a current
        visibility flag.

        Set consumes the tag as TAG_IGNORE. Supply writable tag storage. The
        superclass set result does not confirm that a queued group change
        succeeded.

    SEE ALSO
        MUIA_NListview_HSB_Height, MUIA_NListview_Vert_ScrollBar

NListview.mcc/MUIA_NListview_HSB_Height

    NAME
        MUIA_NListview_HSB_Height -- Read the attached horizontal scrollbar
        height.

    INPUTS
        Get only; LONG pixel dimension returned through IPTR storage.

    FUNCTION
        Return the scrollbar object height when attached, or zero when
        detached. This tests group membership, not whether the window is
        open or the object is currently visible. Read after layout for
        meaningful geometry; queued changes may not yet be applied. A zero
        result alone does not identify the requested mode.

    SEE ALSO
        MUIA_NListview_VSB_Width, MUIA_NListview_Horiz_ScrollBar

NListview.mcc/MUIA_NListview_NList

    NAME
        MUIA_NListview_NList -- Supply or retrieve the embedded list.

    INPUTS
        Initialize/get; Object *. MUIA_Listview_List is a compatibility
        alias.

    FUNCTION
        Supply an NList object or subclass. The own tag takes precedence
        over Listview_List when both are present. An explicitly supplied
        NULL makes construction return NULL; omitting both tags instead
        creates an NList from the remaining constructor tags. The
        constructor modifies the tag list.

        After successful construction, the group hierarchy owns the child
        and disposes it with the container. Get returns a borrowed pointer;
        do not dispose it separately. There is no local setter to replace
        the child.

        For an explicit child, construction forwards these selected values:
        a true Dropable; NList_DragType (preferred to Listview_DragType);
        Listview_Input, MultiSelect, DoubleClick and DefClickColumn (each
        preferred to its NList alias). Each attribute name has the MUIA_
        prefix. A false Dropable does not clear an explicit child's existing
        setting.

        Other child initialization belongs on the child itself. Subsequent
        sets of NList_KeyUpFocus, KeyDownFocus, KeyLeftFocus and
        KeyRightFocus are forwarded. This does not imply general forwarding
        of NList attributes.

        Construction sets the child's NList_KeepActive to this container and
        connects its horizontal-scrollbar notification, which carries
        requests for both axes. Do not share one child between containers.

    RESULT
        Get returns TRUE and the existing child pointer. No new object or
        caller-owned copy is created.

    SEE ALSO
        NList.mcc, NFloattext.mcc

NListview.mcc/MUIA_NListview_Vert_ScrollBar

    NAME
        MUIA_NListview_Vert_ScrollBar -- Request the vertical scrollbar
        mode.

    INPUTS
        Initialize/set/get; LONG. The initial request is
        MUIV_NListview_VSB_Default (5).

    VALUES
        MUIV_NListview_VSB_Always (1), Auto (2), FullAuto (3), None (4),
        Default (5), Left (6). Abbreviated names use the MUIV_NListview_VSB_
        prefix. The first four modes have the same intended policies as the
        horizontal modes. Default reads MUICFG_NListview_VSB and falls back
        to Always. Left requests an always-attached scrollbar before the
        list group.

        AROS resolves every nonzero low-nibble choice to Always, including
        Left and None. Left positioning is therefore not selected by normal
        mode resolution on AROS.

    FUNCTION
        Mode storage, getter timing, tag consumption and deferred updates
        follow Horiz_ScrollBar. Get returns the effective mode, not
        attachment state. MUIV_NListview_VSB_On (0x0030) and VSB_Off
        (0x0010) are the vertical visibility fields. Off is ignored for
        effective Always.

        MUIA_Listview_ScrollerPos is a compatibility input. At construction,
        None requests None on both axes, Left requests vertical Left, and
        Right requests vertical Always; explicit own mode attributes take
        precedence. On set, ScrollerPos translates only to the vertical mode
        (None, Left, Always or Default). These requests remain subject to
        the AROS Always override.

    SEE ALSO
        MUIA_NListview_Horiz_ScrollBar, MUIA_NListview_VSB_Width

NListview.mcc/MUIA_NListview_VSB_Width

    NAME
        MUIA_NListview_VSB_Width -- Read the attached vertical scrollbar
        width.

    INPUTS
        Get only; LONG pixel dimension returned through IPTR storage.

    FUNCTION
        Return the scrollbar object width when attached, or zero when
        detached. This is a membership test, not an on-screen visibility
        test. Read after layout for meaningful geometry and allow queued
        changes to complete. The result does not report the requested or
        effective scrollbar mode.

    SEE ALSO
        MUIA_NListview_HSB_Height, MUIA_NListview_Vert_ScrollBar

NListview.mcc/MUIM_NListview_QueryBeginning

    NAME
        MUIM_NListview_QueryBeginning -- Obsolete query alias.

    SYNOPSIS
        DoMethod(obj, MUIM_NListview_QueryBeginning);

    FUNCTION
        The public header aliases this identifier to
        MUIM_NList_QueryBeginning. NListview handles that method locally; it
        does not forward it to the embedded NList. Do not use this obsolete
        query to obtain a child pointer or an entry position.

    RESULT
        Always zero from the NListview dispatcher.

    SEE ALSO
        MUIA_NListview_NList


